Black's king is standing in a gale around g8 — its shelter has fallen apart. The pressure comes from White's queen on c2 and the rook on d1. Enemy pieces are massing around it, the pawn cover is gone on the f-file, the shield pawn on the h-file has pushed too far forward, the g-file in front of the king is wide open and the back rank is airless. Meanwhile, Black's bishop on h5 is trapped — the cage is shut, and there is no safe square left. It is attacked by White's pawn on g4, and nothing defends it. A capture sequence here wins roughly a minor piece. On top of that, Black's knight on c6 hangs — it stands under attack with no friend in sight. It is under attack from White's bishop on b5. Also, Black's pawn on b7 is wearing two hats — it alone is holding Black's knight on c6 and the rook on a8 together. White's knight on a5 has nowhere to run — the cage is built, and the door is about to close. Meanwhile, Black's rook on a8 has lost its bodyguard — keep half an eye on it.

White's minor pieces have found real work; their counterparts are still waiting for a career. White owns the bishop pair. Black's f8-bishop is biting on granite — its own pawns on c5 and d6 block every road it wants to travel. White's g2-bishop is biting on granite — its own pawns on e4 and f3 block every road it wants to travel. The position is closed, with a locked center. Meanwhile, White's pawn structure is the happier one — the opponent's carries scars that will not heal by themselves. Black has a passed pawn on h4. White has a passed pawn on b5. Black's h5-pawn is isolated. White's a4-pawn is isolated. Black's pawns on f7 and f5 are doubled. White's pawns on c3 and c4 are doubled. Black's d6-pawn is backward. White's d3-pawn is backward. White has a queenside pawn majority. Black has a kingside pawn majority. At the same time, White is ahead on material. White is up the exchange. Beyond that, White's heavy pieces have the open lines to themselves. The d- and e-files are open. The g- and h-files are half-open for Black. The c-file is half-open for White. White has doubled heavy pieces on the d-file. White has a rook on the seventh rank. White owns the squares that matter, and pieces that own squares soon find homes on them. There are holes on d5 and e5 in Black's camp — squares that dream of hosting enemy pieces. There is a hole on e4 in White's camp — a square that dreams of hosting an enemy piece. Black's knight has found a permanent home on e4 and has no intention of leaving. White's knight has found a permanent home on d5 and has no intention of leaving. Meanwhile, White's army breathes freely while the opponent's pieces queue for the same few squares. At the same time, White's pieces are up and working while the opponent's are still putting their boots on. Beyond that, White is asking the questions; the opponent is stuck finding answers.

Here is what White should be dreaming about: keep the position closed — this is knight country, and the bishops know it. Another dream for White: march the queenside majority — those pawns outnumber their opposition and they know it. Another dream for White: put a piece in front of Black's passer and put its dreams on hold (key squares: h3). Another dream for White: double the heavy pieces on the open file and make it their private highway. Another dream for White: keep the extra space and refuse trades — cramped pieces suffer most when nobody leaves the room. Another dream for White: open the position while half the opponent's army is still asleep at home.
